CHANGELOG
---------

[2.1.5]
 * **A `validate_bpf_filter` helper checks a BPF filter without installing
   it.** `add_bpf_filter()` compiles *and installs* a filter on an open
   capture and raises an exception when it cannot. So there was no way to
   test an expression - for example one provided by an end user - without
   a live handle and the (Exception) side effect of applying it. The new
   module-level `packets.core.pcap.validate_bpf_filter(bpf_filter,
   linktype=ETH_EN10MB, snaplen=MAX_SNAPLEN)` compiles the expression
   against a throwaway "dead" handle (`pcap_open_dead`) that opens no
   device or file and captures nothing, then frees the compiled program
   and closes the handle. It returns `(True, None)` when the filter
   compiles and `(False, reason)` otherwise, where `reason` is libpcap's
   own `pcap_geterr` description (for example `"syntax error"`) rather
   than a bare error code. The check is optional: a caller that skips
   it and hands an unchecked filter to `add_bpf_filter()` still gets the
   exception that raises. `linktype` defaults to Ethernet and takes any
   of the module's `ETH_*` constants so the filter is validated against
   the link layer it will really run on.

[2.1.4]
 * **HTTP fixed-length bodies split by the path MTU are decoded, not
   rejected.** A `Content-Length` body can be spread over several TCP
   segments, so a single captured packet often carries only its leading
   part. `HTTPRequest` and `HTTPResponse` used to raise `ValueError('HTTP
   ...: incomplete fixed body')` in that case; they now keep the bytes that
   are present as a partial `body`, leave `data` empty, and set the new
   public `body_complete` flag to false so a caller can tell that stream
   reassembly is needed to see the rest. A fully present body still reports
   `body_complete` true, and the partial fragment still serializes back to
   the exact captured wire. Conflicting or non-numeric `Content-Length`
   headers are still rejected.
 * **HTTP body continuation packets are kept for reassembly, not
   rejected.** When a body is split across TCP segments by the path MTU,
   only the first segment carries the request or response start line; the
   segments holding the body's middle and end have no framing of their own,
   so `Ethernet(pkt_data, l7_ports={80: HTTP})` used to raise
   `ValueError('HTTP ...: missing or incomplete start line')` on them. The
   `HTTP` dispatcher now recognizes a buffer with no parseable start line
   and returns the new `HTTPBodyFragment` message, whose opaque bytes are
   kept verbatim in `data` with `body_complete` false, instead of raising.
   A caller can therefore collect the first (partial) message plus its
   follow-on fragments in capture order and reassemble the whole body
   itself; the library still performs no TCP stream reassembly on its own.
   Every fragment serializes back to its exact captured wire, and the start
   line detection is strict enough that a body line which merely resembles a
   request or `HTTP/` response line is still kept as a fragment.
 * **A `get_http_streams` helper reassembles whole HTTP exchanges from a
   capture.** The decoder is deliberately per-packet and still performs no
   TCP reassembly itself, but the new module-level
   `packets.protos.http.get_http_streams(<packet iterator>)` is the
   caller-side layer that does. Given an iterable of decoded packets (for
   example `Ethernet` frames parsed with `l7_ports={80: HTTP}`), it groups
   them into bidirectional TCP connections, orders each direction by
   sequence number (dropping retransmissions and stopping a contiguous run
   at a missing segment), concatenates the `HTTPRequest`, `HTTPResponse`,
   and `HTTPBodyFragment` payloads back into a continuous stream, and
   re-parses whole request/response messages from it. It returns a dict
   keyed by a string built from the connection's 5-tuple plus a unique id
   derived from both directions' initial sequence numbers -- so a reused
   5-tuple (a fresh connection, delimited by its SYN) does not collide --
   whose value is a list of ordered `(request, response)` `HTTP` pairs
   covering keep-alive and pipelining. A message that could not be fully
   reassembled is still returned with `body_complete` false rather than
   dropped, and packets without TCP or without an HTTP payload are ignored.

[2.1.3]
 * **Addresses are formatted in C rather than through Python.** Reading a
   MAC ran `struct.unpack` and a six conversion `%`-format, reading `IP.src`
   called `socket.inet_ntoa`, and a pcap_query pays that once per column per
   packet. Both now write into a stack buffer: a MAC costs 38ns instead of
   276ns, an IPv4 address 35ns instead of 176ns. Applied to every layer that
   carries an address - Ethernet, ARP, IP, IP6, ICMP6 and IGMP with their
   option and record classes. IPv6 still calls the platform `inet_ntop`
   rather than reimplementing RFC 5952, and anything not exactly 6, 4 or 16
   bytes still goes to the original converter, so error behaviour is
   unchanged.
 * **NetFlow and DHCP share that writer instead of formatting their own.**
   The digit writer was private to `inetpkt.pyx`, which is why those two
   modules each grew a slower IPv4 formatter of their own; it now lives in
   `inetpkt.pxd` as `_fmt_ipv4_buf`, beside the shared parse-buffer readers,
   and takes a buffer pointer so a parser formats straight out of the parse
   buffer with no intermediate bytes. Whole-file v9/IPFIX decode of a 30
   minute capture fell from 152ms to 95ms (763ns to 479ns per record), a
   dense 30 record v5 datagram from 648ns to 224ns per record, and the four
   BOOTP address properties a query reads per packet from 748ns to 95ns.
 * **Frame decode copies less.** Parsing a MAC is one cloned array and a
   memcpy instead of allocating a bytes object to hand to `array()`; the
   Ethernet/IP/UDP fast path passes the datagram body on as a `_null_range`
   instead of copying every body, which is the one thing the owner/offset
   design exists to avoid; and `udp.payload.offset[x:y]` copies only the
   requested window instead of running `pkt2net` over the whole payload,
   `tcp.payload.offset[x:y]` likewise. Full frame decode fell from 510ns to
   405ns, and a decode that also reads both MACs - what a capture query does
   - from 1133ns to 442ns.
 * **pcap_query extracts more columns through compiled descriptors, and
   `frame.*` queries no longer decode at all.** Sixteen TCP and eight IPv6
   fields joined the frame, Ethernet, IPv4 and UDP fields that had
   descriptors, so the most commonly queried columns leave the dynamic
   `get_field_val` route; cost per packet fell 1.50x for one column and
   1.79x to 2.10x for four to sixteen. The dead `field_functions` closures,
   built per query and never called, are gone. A query naming only `frame.*`
   columns skips the `Ethernet(...)` decode - note the deliberate
   consequence that a frame with a complete Ethernet header but a malformed
   IP header now returns its row instead of aborting the query, while a
   frame too short for an Ethernet header, or any query naming a decoded
   field, still raises.
 * Every fast path is pinned against the implementation it replaced.
   `test/test_perf_paths.py` checks the formatters against
   `socket.inet_ntoa`/`inet_ntop` over every octet value in every position,
   all 37 descriptor fields against the value their own layer reports, the
   slice shapes that must keep Python semantics, and both query decode-skip
   cases; `bench/query_fields_bench.py` and `bench/proto_address_bench.py`
   measure the two groups from synthesized traffic. Also removed a redundant
   function local import of `NetflowDecodeContext` in `pcap.pyx`.
 * Seven further opportunities were measured and deliberately not taken.
   NetFlow IPv6 fields and the DHCPv6 relay addresses are capped by the
   `inet_ntop` call RFC 5952 zero compression requires; DNS A/AAAA rdata
   measured 4% over a whole file, since a 3us frame is dominated by label
   parsing; the NetFlow MAC `snprintf` measured no change at all. Of the
   2.1.1 review items, a `Netflow` `_from_owner` hook, avoiding the per
   packet `NetflowDecodeContext` and indexing `resolve_any_template` are all
   worth nothing against an 11us v9 datagram dominated by record decoding -
   and the O(n) registry scan is not on the decode path at all, since
   `_decode_data` always passes a version. Batching offline reads remains
   open and is the largest untaken item.

[2.1.2]
 * **Declared lengths and header offsets now bound the parse.** `IP.total_len`,
   `IP6`'s `payload_len` and `UDP.ulen` were read into fields but never used to
   decide where a layer ended, so the padding every Ethernet NIC adds to a
   short frame was decoded as UDP payload, handed to whatever Layer-7 class was
   registered, and forwarded into NetFlow replay. Parsing now stops at the
   declared length and the bytes behind it are kept on the new read/write
   `PKT.trailer`, so a padded frame still re-serializes byte for byte while
   staying out of the recomputed lengths and checksums; a declared length
   longer than the bytes present is still accepted, so snaplen-truncated
   captures and ICMP-quoted headers read as before. IPv4 `ihl` and TCP
   `data_offset` are validated rather than clamped - `ihl=4` used to decode the
   IPv4 address bytes as a UDP header and `data_offset=0` handed the TCP header
   itself to the Layer-7 parser - and a TCP segment of 9 to 19 bytes raises
   instead of falling through to the keyword defaults and returning a fully
   zeroed layer.
 * **Malformed input fails closed instead of drifting.** The MPLS label stack
   walk is iterative and stops creating layers at `MPLS_MAX_STACK_DEPTH`, so a
   crafted stack no longer trades four bytes of input for a native stack frame,
   and the remainder is preserved as an opaque payload. `IGMPGroupRecord` reads
   `aux_data_len` as the 32-bit word count RFC 3376 4.2.6 defines - which is
   what the MLDv2 record it mirrors already did - and bounds it against the end
   of the IGMP layer, so one such record no longer truncates its aux data,
   desynchronizes the whole group-record list, or returns whatever followed the
   datagram in the frame. A truncated IPv6 extension chain is distinguishable
   from an upper-layer protocol this library does not decode through the new
   readonly `IP6.ext_hdrs_truncated`. `PKT.from_buffer()` copies a caller's
   `array`, so mutation isolation no longer depends on which parse path a layer
   happened to use, and Ethernet forwards `l7_ports` into MPLS so encapsulated
   TCP and UDP reach registered Layer-7 classes.
 * **Serialized header fields describe the bytes actually written.** A computed
   UDP checksum of zero goes out as 0xffff: RFC 768 reserves zero for "no
   checksum sent", so a payload whose ones-complement sum happened to fold to
   zero silently disabled the checksum on IPv4, and over IPv6, where the
   checksum is not optional at all (RFC 8200 8.1), produced an invalid
   datagram. Serializing `IP` with `update` derives `iphl` from the length of
   `options` and `total_len` from the bytes written, instead of emitting a
   header whose own declared length pointed into the middle of it; without
   `update` the caller's values are still emitted untouched, which is how a
   deliberately malformed header is produced.
 * **NetFlow decoding follows the RFCs and the documented fallback.** The
   Enterprise bit RFC 7011 3.2 defines for IPFIX was applied to v9 as well,
   which has no such bit (RFC 3954 8), so a v9 template using the vendor
   range (Cisco ASA NSEL exports 33000-33002 and 40000 and up) lost the top
   bit of its element IDs and consumed 4 bytes of the following field as a
   Private Enterprise Number, failing the whole template set; those exporters
   never had a template learned and every data set they sent fell back to
   `NetflowSimple`. A flowset the parser could not read now dispatches to
   `NetflowSimple` too, rather than returning a partly decoded packet with no
   signal to the caller. Record length now comes from the template, so a short
   all-zero data record is no longer mistaken for the 4-byte set padding
   RFC 3954 5.3 and RFC 7011 3.3.1 allow. v9 and IPFIX reject records that
   belong to no flowset instead of writing a bare header, exceptions raised
   while parsing a datagram reach the caller rather than being printed over a
   half-initialized packet, and the generated record-class cache is bounded in
   least-recently-used order, reachable through
   `template_artifact_cache_info()`,
   `set_template_artifact_cache_limit()` and `clear_template_artifact_cache()`.
 * **One `NetflowSimple`, and replay that respects the version.**
   `packets.protos.netflow` defined a second `NetflowSimple` and assigned it
   over `packets.core.inetpkt.NetflowSimple` at import, which left `isinstance`
   dependent on import order and disabled the core owner/offset Layer-7 fast
   path for NetFlow; the protocol module now re-exports the core class, worth
   about 28% on the Ethernet/IP/UDP/NetFlow decode path. Replay wrote
   `unix_secs` and `unix_nano_seconds` unconditionally, which for IPFIX
   overwrote the sequence number and the observation domain id while leaving
   the real export time stale; both paths now share one helper that advances
   `sys_uptime` for IPFIX, `unix_secs` for v9, and both timestamp fields for
   v1-v8. They also count packets that are not NetFlow, name the first on
   stderr and report the total in the exit status (the BPF filter a replay
   installs constrains only the UDP destination port, so a short datagram used
   to abandon the rest of the capture and a longer one raised part way
   through), and reject a `decode_context` without `force_simple`. The 2055
   overlap between `Netflow` and `NetflowSimple` in `default_ports()` is
   documented.
 * **libpcap failures are visible and handles are not leaked.** `findalldevs()`
   frees the `pcap_if_t` chain `pcap_findalldevs` hands to the caller, so every
   device lookup - including the check `PcapQuery` performs on construction -
   no longer leaks. A `snaplen` of 0 is resolved to the whole frame before the
   open, since only libpcap 1.9 and later reads it as "the maximum" and older
   releases took it literally and captured zero bytes per packet. Calls on a
   closed handle raise instead of passing a NULL `pcap_t` into libpcap, which
   does not check it; the declarations gained exception values along with the
   guards, because a `cpdef int` with no `except` clause cannot propagate one.
   A dumper that cannot be opened raises rather than leaving `dump_hdr_pkt()`
   quietly writing an empty file; `set_snaplen`, `set_promisc` and `set_timeout`
   raise instead of handing back the `PCAP_ERROR_ACTIVATED` libpcap answers for
   an already activated handle, naming the constructor keyword to use; and
   `pcap_info()` scans with the GIL released and no longer reports a deliberate
   `pcap_breakloop` as a read error naming the wrong libpcap function.
 * **Query, CLI and packaging.** `PcapQuery.query(num_packets=N)` returns N
   rows rather than one too many, and names the file it could not open instead
   of formatting the device name, which is `None` on that branch.
   `netflow-player --device` is checked against the devices libpcap reports,
   exposed by the new `packets.core.pcap.list_devices()`; libpcap refusing to
   enumerate them, which is what an unprivileged process sees, is treated as
   "cannot tell" rather than as a failure. Cython is pinned below 3, whose
   defaults for language level, undeclared exception values and `binding`
   differ from the 0.x semantics these extensions rely on; `platforms` now
   lists macOS as well as Linux; and the per-extension `cython_directives`
   dictionaries were removed, since distutils does not know that keyword and
   dropped it with a `UserWarning`, so neither directive had ever been applied.
   Use `cythonize(..., compiler_directives=...)` to apply directives for real.

[2.1.1]
 * Added stateful NetFlow v1/v5/v7/v9 and IPFIX decoding and generation.
 * Added scoped templates, fallback handling, replay support, and faster codecs.

[2.1]
 * **Expanded network protocol support and correctness.** Added IPv6 packets,
   extension headers, MPLS dispatch, and query integration. ICMPv6 now covers
   echo, errors, Neighbor Discovery, MLDv1/v2, option TLVs, and quoted packets,
   preserving unknown or truncated data for round trips. Fixed malformed,
   construction, serialization, and query behavior across ARP, Ethernet, IP,
   ICMP, IGMPv2/v3, MPLS, TCP, UDP, address helpers, and packet headers.
 * **Added and modernized Layer-7 protocols.** Added lossless DHCPv4/DHCPv6
   packets and generic option TLVs, including relay messages, unknown values,
   trailing bytes, transport dispatch, and query fields. Added HTTP/0.9 and
   HTTP/1.x requests and responses with byte-oriented fields, ordered duplicate
   headers, fixed/chunked/close-delimited bodies, trailers, and pipelined
   remainder bytes. DNS parsing and writing were corrected, and the pure-Python
   tutorial implementation works on Python 3 and rejects invalid compression
   pointers instead of looping.
 * **Reworked parsing, construction, and ownership.** Layers now parse bounded
   ranges directly from immutable owner buffers, isolate mutable caller input,
   keep detached children alive, and raise descriptive `ValueError` exceptions
   for truncation instead of reading unchecked data. Parsed layers no longer
   retain redundant raw-packet copies; common keyword constructors use typed
   initializers; and default Layer-7 maps are lazy without shared mutable state.
   Current decoded operations measure 2.23x to 3.26x faster than the pre-2.1
   baseline, with further construction and Layer-7 gains from specialization.
 * **Rebuilt serialization around a shared writer.** `pkt2net()` now streams
   nested layers into one growable buffer and patches lengths, record sizes, and
   checksums in place, preserving unchanged wire output while avoiding layer-by-
   layer temporary objects. Representative packet serialization is about 2x to
   5.5x faster, and DNS writing is about 2.2x to 3.3x faster. Update flags no
   longer rewrite checksums or lengths inside quoted ICMPv6 packets.
 * **Hardened and accelerated libpcap integration.** Capture handles and dumpers
   are released on collection, stored names own their bytes, and `pcap_t` is
   declared opaque. Open, read, filter, and send failures now reach callers;
   `pcap_info()` handles empty files; blocking libpcap calls release the GIL;
   and bounded dispatch/direct-decode paths reduce callbacks, copies, and
   intermediate packet objects without exposing borrowed libpcap storage.
 * **Corrected and specialized `PcapQuery`.** Fields are registered and matched
   by complete name, TCP/UDP payload-offset slices work again, unsupported
   fields are reported consistently, and live captures wait through timeouts
   until an explicit stop condition. Common fields use compiled descriptors
   while uncommon and custom fields retain dynamic fallback, improving repeated
   representative queries by about 12% to 24%.
 * **Made NetFlow tools and project documentation release-ready.** Replay now
   rewrites the first packet consistently, validates short payloads, reports
   send failures and empty selections, and avoids repeated per-packet setup.
   `netflow-player` validates required files, devices, addresses, MACs, and
   numeric ranges, honors supplied `argv`, and returns usage errors instead of
   tracebacks. README, API references, and the tutorial now describe the
   shipping `packets` namespace, libpcap build, constructors, constants,
   readers/writers, queries, and supported protocols; the package version is
   2.1 and the new compiled protocol modules are included in the build.

